  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1215 PM CDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\nShowers with a few embedded storms continue through this afternoon \nand evening. The upper-level pattern shows a broad ridge over the \nSoutheast with troughing over the PacNW. This puts the Central \nPlains under a strong southwesterly flow regime, with shortwaves \ncontinuing to trigger showers and storms along and just north of a \nfrontal boundary currently over far southeast Nebraska and southwest \nIowa. As we go through the day today, showers and storms will \ngradually shift south with the frontal boundary with skies becoming \npartly to mostly sunny over far northeast Nebraska. Temperature will \nstay on the cooler side today with highs in the upper 60s to low 70s.\n\nA developing low pressure system on the lee side of the Rockies in \neastern Colorado will bring an upper-level warm front back north \nthrough our area on Wednesday leading to more chances for showers \nand storms Wednesday morning tapering off Wednesday \nafternoon from west to east. While this will bring back \nsoutherly flow, cloud cover will limit temperatures with highs \nstaying in the low-to-mid 70s Wednesday afternoon. \n\nRepeated rounds of locally heavy rainfall will only increase the\npotential for flooding through Wednesday. A Flood Watch\ncontinues until Wednesday afternoon as conditions remain \nfavorable for locally heavy rainfall and flash flooding. \n\nThursday into Thursday night the low pressure system tracks \nnortheast across west-central Nebraska, pulling the surface front \nnorth across our area. Additional showers and storms will be \npossible as this front moves through with temperatures warming into \nthe low-to-mid 80s south of the front, and mid 70s north where the \nfront doesn't arrive until later in the day. Shear is really weak \nalong this frontal boundary, meaning little-to-no severe weather \nthreat with more disorganized scattered convection. \n\nA strong southwesterly low-level jet develops Thursday night into \nearly Friday advecting in drier air from west Texas. This will bring \ndown rain chances during the day on Friday and could limit shower \nand storm coverage across our western counties Friday night into \nearly Saturday as another shortwave interacts with the dry line \ndraped across our area. Best chances will be Omaha-Nebraska City \neastward (60-70%) while farther west rain chances will be more 30-\n45%. This may be our next chance for some stronger storms as NSSL \nMachine Learning guidance highlights this time period. NAM, GFS, and \nECWMF struggle to line up storms with the better 0-1km and 0-3km \nshear, but if these come into alignment we could see stronger \nstorms, mainly with large hail and damaging winds as hazards Friday \nevening. \n\nWe remain in this active southwesterly flow pattern going into the \nweekend, with details becoming more uncertain as fine details become \nmore difficult for our longer-range models to pick out. Temperatures \nmay cool back down through the weekend with additional periodic \nchances for showers and storms, nothing looking particularly severe \nat this time. Guidance diverges more significantly going into next \nweek with potential for us to break out of this persistent \nsouthwesterly flow regime. This could mean drier, seasonably cooler \nweather to start next week.",
